          %h2#description Description
          %p
            In their 1939 paper, Myers and Steenrod proved two theorems on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifolds"} Riemannian manifolds
          %p The first theorem shows that distance-preserving maps between Riemannian manifolds are differentiable isometries.
          %p
            In 1956 Palais simplified the proof and extended the result to show that a Riemannian manifold can be reconstructed from its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} fully faithful functor
          %p
            The second theorem proves that the group of isometries of a Riemannian manifold is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Recall that an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometry"} isometry
            between two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifolds"} Riemannian manifolds
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo ,
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M,g), (N,h)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi M
                  %mo →
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: M \to N
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo *
                    %mi h
                    %mo =
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^* h = g
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            respects the Riemannian structure as well as the differentiable structure.
          %p
            It follows that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d,d'
            are the metrics on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M,N
            induced by the Riemannian metrics
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g,h
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d'(\phi(x),\phi(y)) = d(x,y)
            for
            = succeed "\u2014that" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y \in M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            is distance-preserving. Interestingly, a version of the converse is true, according to the Myers–Steenrod theorem:
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem_myerssteenrod Theorem (Myers–Steenrod)
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi M
                    %mo →
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: M \to N
              is distance-preserving and surjective, then it is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometry"} isometry
              (in particular, it is smooth).
          %p
            For notational simplicity, assume
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo =
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N=M
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            is evidently a homeomorphism. Now pick
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in M
            and a neighborhood
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_r(p)
            such that for any
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi r
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q \in D_r(p)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p,q
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \circ \gamma
            is a geodesic in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geodesic"} geodesic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma
            can be assumed to be parametrized by unit length. We have for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(p,q) = d(\gamma(t),p) + d(\gamma(t), q).
          %p Thus
          #eq:streq.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(\phi(p),\phi(q)) = d(\phi(\gamma(t)),\phi(p)) + d(\phi(\gamma(t)), \phi(q)) .
          %p In other words, we have strict equality in the triangle inequality.
          %h2#geodesic_preservation Geodesic preservation
          %p
            In a Riemannian manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo ,
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q,r
            lie in a small neighborhood of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi q
                  %mo ,
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi r
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi q
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(q,r) + d(r,p)  = d(q,p) ,
          %p
            then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            lies on a geodesic betwen
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p,q
            = succeed "Indeed," do
              %br/
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            and a geodesic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            that travel at unit speed and minimze the distances; we can do this locally even without the assumption of completeness. The catenation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \beta
            minimizes the length from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            lies on the geodesic.
          %p
            So, returning to the discussion above, we see that by
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:streq"} (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            = succeed "." do
              %em sends geodesics to geodesics
          %h2#exponential_coordinates Exponential coordinates
          %p
            Now fix open
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset T_p(M),V \subset T_q(N)
            containing the origin such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi exp
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp_p
            takes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            diffeomorphically to a neighborhood
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi r
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_r(p)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi exp
                    %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp_q
            takes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            diffeomorphically to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi r
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_r(q)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            as a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{\phi}: U \to V
            in these exponential coordinates.
            %br>/
            It is obtained as follows: if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in U
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi γ
                    %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma_v
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi γ
                    %mi v
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma_v'(0) = v
            and consider
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi γ
                      %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\phi \circ \gamma_v)'(0)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{\phi}: T_p(M) \to T_q(M).
          %p
            Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi γ
                    %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma_v
            moves at constant speed
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |v|
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi γ
                    %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \circ \gamma_v
            moves at the same speed, it follows that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{\phi}
            is norm-preserving. Also
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{\phi}(0) = 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{\phi}
            is linear, then we’ll get smoothness in this exponential coordinate system—hence smoothness in general.
          %h2#_is_an_isometry
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{\phi}
            is an isometry
          %p It can be checked that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %menclose{:notation => "box"}
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mi lim
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi B
                          %mo →
                          %mn 0
                          %mo ∈
                          %msub
                            %mi T
                            %mi p
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi M
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi exp
                            %mi p
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi exp
                            %mi p
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi A
                          %mo −
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \boxed{\lim_{A,B \to 0 \in T_p(M)} \frac{d(\exp_p(A),\exp_p(B)) }{|A-B|} } = 1.
          %p We will postpone this for now.
          %p
            This is what we will use to show that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{\phi}: T_p(M) \to T_q(M)
            is an isometry, where each tangent space is given the norm from the Riemannian metric. Pick
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B \in T_p(M)
            and consider the geodesics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi γ
                    %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi γ
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma_{A},\gamma_{B}
            at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi γ
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi γ
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma_{A'}, \gamma_{B'}
            where
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ′
                    %mo =
                    %mover
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo ′
                    %mo =
                    %mover
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A'=\tilde{\phi}(A), B' = \tilde{\phi}(B)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi γ
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi γ
                      %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi γ
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi γ
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                        %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(\gamma_{A}(t), \gamma_{B}(t)) = d( \gamma_{A'}(t), \gamma_{B'}(t))
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo →
                      %mn 0
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi exp
                        %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi exp
                        %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi A
                      %mo −
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo →
                      %mn 0
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi exp
                        %mi q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mi A
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi exp
                        %mi q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mi B
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi A
                      %mo −
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi A
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi B
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi A
                      %mo −
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  1=  \lim_{t \to 0} \frac{d(\exp_p(t A),\exp_p(t B)) }{t|A-B|}
                  \=  \lim_{t \to 0} \frac{d(\exp_q(t A'),\exp_q(t B')) }{t|A-B|}  = \frac{|A'-B'|}{|A-B|}.
          %p
            To show that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{\phi}
            is linear, we appeal to a general fact:
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X,Y
            be normed linear spaces and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T: X \to Y
            a map such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi Tx
                  %mo −
                  %mi Tx
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo −
                  %mi x
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |Tx - Tx'|  = |x-x'|
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,x' \in X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is linear if
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(0) = 0
          %h2#conclusion_of_the_proof Conclusion of the proof
          %p
            Now we have seen that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            is smooth, and that for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in T_p(M)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi v
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi p
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |v|_p = |\phi_*(v)|_{\phi(p)}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            preserves lengths on tangent vectors. By the polarization identity,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            preserves the inner product, and is thus an isometry.
          %h2#a_lemma A lemma
          %p We never proved a fact about the exponential map—the equality
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo →
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi exp
                        %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi exp
                        %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi A
                      %mo −
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lim_{A,B \to 0 \in T_p(M)} \frac{d(\exp_p(A),\exp_p(B)) }{|A-B|} = 1.
          %p
            We will briefly sketch the idea here.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi A
                  %mo −
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |A-B|
            is the length of the linear path from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_p(M)
          .un_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c:(0,1) \to T_p(M)
              is a path in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_p(M)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c((0,1)) \to 0
              with the derivative
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi c
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |c'|
              staying bounded,
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi l
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi exp
                          %mi p
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi l
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{ l(\exp_p(c))}{l(c)} = 1.
          %p We are abusing notation quite a bit here, but it should not cause confusion.
          %p The reason is that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi l
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo ∫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi c
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l(c) = \int |c'|
          %p while
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi l
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi exp
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo ∫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi exp
                    %mi p
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi c
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi exp
                        %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l(\exp_p(c)) = \int | (\exp_p)_{*c(t)} c'(t) |_{\exp_p(c(t))}
          %p
            where in the second equation we are referring to the norms induced by the metric on the various tangent spaces of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi o
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi l
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} o(l(c))
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi exp
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp_p
            has derivative the identity at
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \in T_p(M)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi exp
                        %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{\exp_p(c(t))}(M)
            is very close (say by a factor of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ±
                    %mi ϵ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \pm \epsilon
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_p(M)
            if we work in local coordinates and agree to identify the tangent spaces.
